== Chapter XII

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[The #smallcaps[eighth] direction: Thinking much on the excellency of the majesty of God — How little we are acquainted with him, set out and considered.]

==== Eighth Direction: Meditations That Fill the Soul with Self-Abasement

#smallcaps[Eighth], use and exercise yourself in such meditations as may serve to fill you at all times with #emph[self-abasement] and thoughts of your own vileness. For example:

1\. Think much on the #emph[excellency] of the majesty of God and your infinite, inconceivable distance from him. Many thoughts of it cannot help but fill you with a sense of your own vileness, and that strikes deep at the root of any indwelling sin. When Job comes to a clear sight of the greatness and excellency of God, he is filled with self-abhorrence and pressed down to humiliation (Job 42:5, 6). And into what state does the prophet Habakkuk say he was cast when he grasped the majesty of God? (ch. 3:16). “Awesome majesty surrounds Him,” says Job.#footnote[Job 37:22.] This is why the people of old thought that when they had seen God they would die. The Scripture is full of this self-abasing consideration. In respect of God it compares the men of the earth to “grasshoppers,” to “emptiness,” to “a speck of dust on the scales.”#footnote[Isa 40:12–25.] Be much in thoughts of this kind, to bring down the pride of your heart and to keep your soul humble within you. Nothing will make you less open to being taken in by the deceits of sin than such a frame of heart. Think greatly of the greatness of God.

2\. Think much of how #emph[unacquainted] you are with him. Though you know enough to keep you low and humble, how small a portion of him it is that you know! The thought of this cast that wise man into the view of himself which he expresses in Prov 30:2–4: “Surely I am the most ignorant of men, and I lack the understanding of a man. I have not learned wisdom, and I have no knowledge of the Holy One. Who has ascended to heaven and come down? Who has gathered the wind in His hands? Who has bound up the waters in His cloak? Who has established all the ends of the earth? What is His name, and what is the name of His Son—surely you know!” Labor with this too, to bring down the pride of your heart. What do you know of God? How small a portion it is! How immense he is in his nature! Can you look into the abyss of eternity without terror? You cannot bear the rays of his glorious being.

Because I regard this consideration as of great use in our walk with God, as far as it is consistent with the boldness of children that is given us in Jesus Christ to draw near to the throne of grace, I will dwell on it further, to leave a lasting impression of it on the souls of those who desire to walk humbly with God.

Consider, then, I say, to keep your heart in continual awe of the majesty of God, that those of the highest and most eminent attainments, of the nearest and most familiar communion with God, still know in this life only very little of him and his glory. God reveals his name to Moses, the most glorious attributes he has shown in the covenant of grace (Exod 34:5, 6); yet all of it is only God’s “back.” All that Moses knows by it is little and low compared with the perfections of God’s glory. That is why it is said with special reference to Moses, “No one has ever seen God” (John 1:18). He speaks of Moses in comparison with Christ in verse 17, and of him it is said here, “No one,” not even Moses, the most eminent among them, “has ever seen God.” We speak much of God; we can talk of him, his ways, his works, his counsels, all day long. The truth is, we know very little of him. Our thoughts, our meditations, our expressions of him are low, many of them unworthy of his glory, none of them reaching his perfections.

You#super[+] will say that Moses was under the law, when God wrapped himself in darkness, and his mind in types and clouds and dark institutions. But under the glorious shining of the gospel, which has brought life and immortality to light, God has been revealed from his own bosom. So we now know him much more clearly, and as he is; we now see his #emph[face], and not only his #emph[back], as Moses did.

#emph[Ans.] 1. I acknowledge a vast and almost inconceivable difference between the acquaintance we now have with God, since he has spoken to us by his own Son,#footnote[Heb 1:2.] and that which the saints in general had under the law. Their eyes were as good, sharp, and clear as ours, their faith and spiritual understanding not behind ours, and the object was as glorious to them as to us. Yet our day is clearer than theirs was: the clouds are blown away and scattered,#footnote[Song 4:6.] the shadows of the night are gone and fled, the sun is risen, and the means of sight are made more excellent and clear than before. Yet:

2\. The particular sight Moses had of God in Exod 34 was a #emph[gospel sight], a sight of God as “gracious,” and so on; and yet it is called only his “back,” that is, low and poor compared with his excellencies and perfections.

3\. The apostle exalts to the utmost this glory of light above that of the law, and shows that now the “veil” that caused darkness is taken away, so that with “unveiled” or uncovered “faces#footnote[Ἀνακεκαλυμμένῳ προσώπῳ.] we all behold the glory of the Lord.” Yet he tells us how: “as in a mirror” (2 Cor 3:18). “In a mirror”: how is that? Clearly, perfectly? Alas, no! He tells you how it is in 1 Cor 13:12: “Now we see but a dim reflection as in a mirror,” he says. The apostle is not speaking of a telescope that helps us see things far off; and yet even such helps, how poor they are! How far we fall short of the truth of things, for all their help! It is a looking glass he alludes to (where there are only dim likenesses and images of things, not the things themselves), and he compares our knowledge to the sight we have in it. He also tells us that all we do see, δι’ ἐσόπτρου, “by” or “through this mirror,” is in αἰνίγματι, “in a riddle,” in darkness and obscurity. And speaking of himself, who was surely far more clear-sighted than anyone now living, he tells us that he saw only ἐκ μέρους, “in part.” He saw only the back of heavenly things (verse 12), and he compares all the knowledge of God he had attained to the knowledge he had of things when he was a child (verse 11). It is a μέρος [part], short of τὸ τέλειον [the perfect]; indeed, it is such as καταργηθήσεται, “it will be destroyed,” or done away. We know what weak, feeble, uncertain notions and ideas children have of anything hard to grasp; and how, when they grow up with some improvement in their gifts and abilities, those ideas vanish and they are ashamed of them. It is to a child’s credit that he loves, honors, believes, and obeys his father; but as for his knowledge and notions, his father knows their childishness and folly. For all our confidence in high attainments, all our notions of God are only childish compared with his infinite perfections. We lisp and babble, and for the most part say we know not what, in what we think are our most accurate conceptions and notions of God. We may love, honor, believe, and obey our Father, and with that he accepts our childish thoughts, for they are only childish. We see only his back; we know only a little of him. Hence the promise that so often supports and comforts us in our distress: “We will see Him as He is”; we will see him “face to face”; we will “know fully, even as” we are “fully known,” and “take hold of that for which” we were taken hold of (1 Cor 13:12, 1 John 3:2). And, expressly: “Now we do not see Him.” All of this shows that here we see only his back; not as he is, but in a dark, obscure representation; not in the perfection of his glory.

The queen of Sheba had heard much of Solomon, and had formed many great thoughts of his magnificence in her mind from it; but when she came and saw his glory, she had to confess that not even half the truth had been told her. We may suppose that here we have attained great knowledge, clear and high thoughts of God; but alas! when he brings us into his presence we will cry out, “We never knew him as he is; not a thousandth part of his glory, perfection, and blessedness ever entered our hearts.”

The apostle tells us in 1 John 3:2 that we do not know what we ourselves will be, what we will find ourselves to be in the end; much less will it enter our hearts to conceive what God is, and what we will find him to be. Consider either him who is to be known, or the way by which we know him, and this will appear further:

(1.) We know so little of #emph[God] because it is #emph[God] who is to be known; that is, he who has described himself to us very much by this, that we #emph[cannot] know him. What else does he mean when he calls himself invisible, incomprehensible, and the like? That is, he whom we do not and cannot know as he is. And our further progress consists more in knowing what he is not than what he is. So he is described as immortal, infinite; that is, he is not mortal, finite, and limited, as we are. Hence that glorious description of him in 1 Tim 6:16: “He alone is immortal and dwells in unapproachable light. No one has ever seen Him, nor can anyone see Him.” His light is such that no creature can approach it. He is not seen, not because he cannot be seen, but because we cannot bear the sight of him. The light of God, in whom there is no darkness, forbids all access to him by any creature whatever. We who cannot look at the sun in its glory are too weak to bear the beams of infinite brightness. On this account, as was said, the wise man professes himself “the most ignorant of men,” no better than a beast, and says he lacks “the understanding of a man” (Prov 30:2). That is, he knew nothing compared with God, so that he seemed to have lost all his understanding once he came to consider him, his work, and his ways.

Let our souls come down in this consideration to some particulars:

\[1.\] As for the #emph[being] of God: we are so far from knowing it well enough to teach one another about it in words and expressions, that to form any conception of it in our minds, with such likenesses and impressions of things as we receive the knowledge of all other things by, is to make an idol for ourselves, and so to worship a god of our own making, and not the God who made us. We may as well and as lawfully carve him out of wood or stone as form a being for him in our minds, suited to our understanding. The utmost of our best thoughts of the being of God is that we can have no thoughts of it. Our knowledge of a being is low indeed when it rises no higher than to know that we do not know it.

\[2.\] There are #emph[some] things of God which he himself has taught us to speak of, and has given us rules for our expressions of them. But when we have done so, we do not see the things themselves; we do not know them. To #emph[believe] and #emph[admire] is all we attain to. We profess, as we are taught, that God is infinite, omnipotent, eternal; and we know what disputes and notions there are about omnipresence, immensity, infiniteness, and eternity. We have, I say, words and notions about these things; but as for the things themselves, what do we know? What do we grasp of them? Can the mind of man do anything more than swallow itself up in an infinite abyss, which is as nothing, and give itself up to what it cannot conceive, much less express? Is not our understanding like a beast’s in the contemplation of such things, as if it were not there at all? Indeed, the perfection of our understanding is not to understand, and to rest there. It is only the back of eternity and infiniteness that we catch a glimpse of. What shall I say of the Trinity, the subsistence of distinct persons in the same individual essence? It is a mystery denied by many because understood by none, a mystery whose every letter is mysterious. Who can declare the generation of the Son, the procession of the Spirit, or the difference between the one and the other? But I will not give further instances. The infinite and inconceivable distance between him and us keeps us in the dark as to any sight of his face or clear understanding of his perfections.

We know him by what he does rather than by what he is, by his doing us good rather than by his essential goodness; and, as Job says, how small a portion of him is revealed by this!

(2.) We know little of God because it is by #emph[faith] alone that we know him here. I will not now discuss the impressions that remain by nature on the hearts of all men that there is a God, nor what they may rationally be taught about that God from the works of his creation and providence, which they see and behold. It is admitted, and that from the sad experience of all ages, that this knowledge is so weak, low, dark, and confused that none ever glorified God as they ought on that account. Instead, for all their knowledge of God, they were in fact “without God in the world.”

The chief, and practically the only, acquaintance we have with God and his dealings is by faith. “Anyone who approaches Him must believe that He exists and that He rewards those who earnestly seek Him” (Heb 11:6). Our knowledge of him and of his rewarding (the foundation of our obedience, or our coming to him) is believing. “We walk by faith, not by sight” (2 Cor 5:7): Διὰ πίστεως οὐ διὰ εἴδους, by faith, and so by faith as not to have any distinct idea, image, or likeness of what we believe. Faith is all the evidence we have of “what we do not see” (Heb 11:1). I might dwell here on its nature, and show from all that goes with it and concerns it that we know only the back of what we know by faith alone. As to its origin, it is built purely on the testimony of him whom we have not seen; as the apostle says, “How can you#super[+] love him whom you#super[+] have not seen?” That is, whom you#super[+] know to exist only by faith. Faith receives everything on #emph[his] testimony, and receives that he exists only on his own testimony. As to its nature, it is assent upon testimony, not evidence upon demonstration; and its object is, as was said before, above us. That is why our faith, as was noted before, is called seeing “a dim reflection as in a mirror.” All that we know this way (and all that we know of God we know this way) is only low, dark, and obscure.

But you#super[+] will say: “All this is true, but perhaps it is true only of those who do not know God as he is revealed in Jesus Christ. With those who do, it is otherwise. It is true that ‘no one has ever seen God,’ but ‘the one and only Son … has made Him known’ (John 1:18); and ‘the Son of God has come and has given us understanding, so that we may know Him who is true’ (1 John 5:20). The light of ‘the gospel of the glory of Christ, who is the image of God,’ shines on believers (2 Cor 4:4). Indeed, ‘God, who said, “Let light shine out of darkness,” made His light shine in our hearts to give us the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ’ (verse 6). So though ‘we were once darkness,’ we are now ‘light in the Lord’ (Eph 5:8). And the apostle says, ‘We … with unveiled faces all behold the glory of the Lord’ (2 Cor 3:18). We are now so far from being in such darkness, or at such a distance from God, that our ‘fellowship … is with the Father and with His Son’ (1 John 1:3). The light of the gospel by which God is now revealed is glorious; not a star, but the sun in his beauty has risen on us, and the veil is taken from our faces. So though unbelievers, and perhaps some weak believers, may be in some darkness, those of any growth or considerable attainments have a clear sight and view of the face of God in Jesus Christ.”

To which I answer:

\[1.\] The truth is, we all know #emph[enough] of him to love him more than we do, to delight in him, serve him, believe him, obey him, and trust in him beyond anything we have yet attained. Our darkness and weakness are no excuse for our negligence and disobedience. Who has ever lived up to the knowledge he has had of the perfections, excellencies, and will of God? God’s purpose in giving us any knowledge of himself here is that we may “glorify Him as God”: that is, love him, serve him, believe and obey him, and give him all the honor and glory that poor sinful creatures owe to a sin-pardoning God and Creator. We must all acknowledge that we have never been thoroughly transformed into the image of the knowledge we have had. And had we used our talents well, we might have been trusted with more.

\[2.\] #emph[Comparatively], the knowledge we have of God by the revelation of Jesus Christ in the gospel is exceedingly eminent and glorious. It is so in comparison with any knowledge of God that might be attained otherwise, or that was delivered in the law under the Old Testament, which had only the shadow of good things, not their express image. The apostle pursues this at length in 2 Cor 3. In these last days Christ has revealed the Father from his own bosom, declared his name, and made known his mind, will, and counsel in a far clearer, more eminent, more distinct way than he did before, while he kept his people under the schooling of the law. And this, for the most part, is what the passages mentioned above mean. The clear, plain delivery and declaration of God and his will in the gospel is expressly exalted above every other way in which he has revealed himself.

\[3.\] The difference between believers and unbelievers in knowledge lies not so much in #emph[the matter of their knowledge] as in #emph[the manner of knowing]. Some unbelievers may know more, and be able to say more, of God, his perfections, and his will than many believers. But they know nothing as they ought, nothing in the right way, nothing spiritually and savingly, nothing with a holy, heavenly light. The excellency of a believer is not that he has a wide grasp of things, but that what he does grasp, which may perhaps be very little, he sees in the light of the Spirit of God, in a saving, soul-transforming light. And this is what gives us communion with God, not prying thoughts or curiously raised notions.

\[4.\] By his word and Spirit, Jesus Christ reveals God to the hearts of all his people as a Father, as a God in covenant, as a rewarder, in every way enough to teach us to obey him here and to lead us to his bosom, to lie down there in the enjoyment of him for eternity. But still,

\[5.\] For all this, it is only a #emph[little portion] of him that we know; we see only his back. For:

1#emph[st]. The aim of all gospel revelation is not to #emph[unveil God’s essential glory] so that we should see him as he is. It is only to declare as much of him as he knows to be enough as a foundation for our faith, love, obedience, and coming to him; that is, for the faith he expects from us here, and such service as befits poor creatures in the midst of temptations. But when he calls us to eternal admiration and contemplation, without interruption, he will make a new kind of discovery of himself, and the whole shape of things as it now lies before us will pass away like a shadow.

2#emph[dly]. We are dull and slow of heart to receive what is revealed in the word. God, through our weakness and infirmity, keeps us in continual dependence on him for teaching and for revelations of himself from his word, and never in this world brings any soul to the utmost of what can be drawn out of the word and discovered in it. So although the way of revelation in the gospel is clear and evident, we know little of the things themselves that are revealed.

Let us, then, return to the use and purpose of this consideration. Will not a right sense of this inconceivable greatness of God, and of the infinite distance at which we stand from him, fill the soul with a holy and reverent fear of him, and so keep it in a frame unsuited to the thriving or flourishing of any lust whatever? Let the soul be continually accustomed to reverent thoughts of God’s greatness and omnipresence, and it will be very much on its guard against any unfitting conduct. Consider him with whom you#super[+] have to do: “our God is a consuming fire.” And in your#super[+] deepest abasement at his presence and his eye, know that your#super[+] very nature is too narrow to bear thoughts fit for his essential glory.
